Kernel interface
****************

   There are several different method to read kernel routing table
information, update kernel routing table, and look up interfaces.

`ioctl'
     `ioctl' method is very traditional way for reading/writing kernel
     information.  `ioctl' can be used for looking up interface and
     get/set interface address, flags, mtu and other information.  Also
     `ioctl' can insert and delete kernel routing table information.  It
     will available on almost platform which zebra supports but it is
     little bit ugly method so if more better method is supported by
     the kernel zebra uses that.

`sysctl'
     `sysctl' can lookup kernel information by MIB (Management
     Information Base) syntax.  Normally it only provide a way of
     getting information from the kernel.  So one want to change kernel
     information the other method such as `ioctl' will be required.

`proc filesystem'
     `proc filesystem' provide easy way of getting kernel information.

`routing socket'

`netlink'

File: zebra.info,  Node: Linux netlink,  Next: Zebra protocol,  Prev: Kernel interface,  Up: Top

Linux netlink
*************

   On recent Linux kernel (2.0.x and 2.2.x), there is a kernel/user
communication support which called `netlink'.  It can make it possible
asynchronous communication between kernel and Zebra such like routing
socket over BSD systems.

   Before you use this feature, you are sure to select kernel support
option 'Kernel/User network link driver' and 'Routing messages'.

   Today, /dev/route special device file is obsolete.  Netlink
communication is done by read/write over netlink socket.

   After the kernel configuration, please reconfigure and make install
Zebra.  You can use netlink as a dynamic routing update channel between
Zebra and kernel.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Zebra protocol,  Next: Copying,  Prev: Linux netlink,  Up: Top

Zebra protocol
**************

   Zebra provides standard interface for updating kernel routing table
and interface look up method.  If you want to write your own routing
protocol Zebra protocol is very useful to you.  There is no need of
knowing the detail of ugly OS interface.  You only send essential
information to `zebra' daemon.

   Below is the list of Zebra protocol type.

     ZEBRA_IPV4_ROUTE_ADD
     ZEBRA_IPV4_ROUTE_DELETE
     ZEBRA_IPV6_ROUTE_ADD
     ZEBRA_IPV6_ROUTE_DELETE
     ZEBRA_GET_ALL_INTERFACE
     ZEBRA_GET_ONE_INTERFACE
     ZEBRA_GET_HOSTINFO


